    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's an updated and revised set of critical reviewer comments that delve deeper into the technical aspects of the paper:\n\nUpon examining the Time-Trans architecture's ablation studies, I am concerned that the proposed simplicity gains from dilating the temporal attention mechanism may be at the expense of frequency domain modality. Specifically, the shift from a standard full-bandwidth attention mechanism to a dilated version, with its inefficiencies in handling periodic signals, raises questions regarding the suitability of this approach for capturing subtle patterns in time-series data that are inherently compressible in the frequency domain. Furthermore, the theoretical justification for the reduced computational complexity of O(n log n) relies on a simplified model of attention filtering, which assumes an i.i.d. distribution over frequency bins – a potentially unjustified assumption, given the temporal dependencies and correlations present in many real-world time-series datasets.\n\nThis critique is more expansive, technical, and intent on highlighting specific vulnerabilities and weaknesses in the methodology rather than being superficial.",
      "source": "target",
