    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's an expanded and revised set of critical reviewer comments, divided into sections for clarity and rigor:\n\n**Methodology**\n\nThe proposed Time-Trans architecture's ablation studies seem to hinge on the assumption that dilating the temporal attention mechanism reduces the computational complexity to O(n log n). However, this assumption relies on a simplistic model of attention filtering, where each frequency bin is treated independently. However, recent work in kernel-based neural networks and frequency-domain attention mechanisms suggests that temporal dependencies can lead to interactions between frequency bins, potentially invalidating this assumption.\n\nFurthermore, the dilation scheme seems to overlook the impact of downsampling on the frequency domain representation. Even if the filtering mechanism reduces the computational complexity, the associated loss of frequency resolution may introduce new artifacts and reduce the model's ability to capture subtle patterns in the time series.\n\n**Theoretical Grounding**\n\nThe complexity analysis relies on the i.i.d. assumption, which is clearly enervating given the temporal dependencies present in many time-series datasets. This oversimplification neglects the linear dependencies between observations, which are fundamental to the efficient estimation of many time-series models. In fact,_recursive linear projections (RLP) models have established that more flexible linear estimators (e.g., linear Gaussian processes",
      "source": "target",
